{"_data": [{"contents": [{"content": "", "type": "lesson_name"}, {"content": "Issues in Data Validation", "type": "unit_name"}, {"content": 5, "type": "position"}, {"content": "stub", "type": "origin"}, {"content": 3, "type": "lesson"}, {"content": "A2.1", "type": "level"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"content": "false", "type": "test"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"content": "These are words you use to talk about issues in data validation.", "type": "text"}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Describe common activities at work to a visitor using short sentences in familiar spoken and written situations.  Understand information about data and its structure in simple spoken and written situations.", "type": "primary"}, {"content": "Discusses a range of options for potential actions at work through the correct use of grammar, vocabulary; level of formality and purpose, and cohesion and coherence aspects appropriate to this level.  Exchanges simple information on the data validation and data warehouses.", "type": "secondary_1"}], "type": "objectives"}, {"content": "enpe7n3le03ob05re1img01.jpg", "type": "background_image"}, {"contents": [{"content": "General and Specific Determiners ", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "<strong>Determiners</strong> are words, such as my, his, the, other, etc., that go at the beginning of a noun phrase. We use them to determine if the noun phrase is specific or general. ", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "When it is clear which thing(s) or person/people you are talking about, you use <strong>specific determiners.</strong></br> </br>  The most common specific determiners are:", "type": "text"}, {"content": "{\"rows\":5,\"columns\":2,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"Specific determiners\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\" \\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<strong>the definite article</strong>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"the\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<strong>possessives</strong>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"my, your, his, her, its, our, their, whose\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<strong>demonstratives</strong>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"this, that, these, those\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<strong>interrogatives</strong>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"which\\\"}\"],\"styles\":\" intro_table_border instruction_table_background\"}", "type": "table"}, {"content": "<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Example:</p> <p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><i>Oops, I didn&rsquo;t set <b>the</b> proper validation rules to <b>the</b> textbox, <b>my</b> bad</i>.</p><br/><br/>Use <strong>general determiners</strong> to talk about how much stuff or how many things or people you're talking about. </br>  The general determiners are: ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "{\"rows\":2,\"columns\":1,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<p style align = \\\"center\\\">General determiners</p>\\\"}\"],\"styles\":\" intro_table_border instruction_table_background\"}", "type": "table"}, {"content": "When users enter data into any data field of a program or app, the data validation rules try to identify the errors the users are making.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "Although it is a highly effective process, any time a user enters data manually, there is a slight chance that he makes an error. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": " An example of this kind of error might be entering \u201cKile\u201d instead of \u201cKyle.\u201d ", "type": "example"}, {"content": " You can also enter the data twice and check it on the screen against the original paper document.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "{\"rows\":1,\"columns\":4,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"a few<br/> a little<br/>  all<br/>  another<br/>  any\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"both<br/>  each<br/>  either<br/>  enough<br/>  every\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"few<br/>  fewer<br/>  less<br/>  little<br/>  many<br/> more<br/>  most<br/>  much\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"neither<br/>  no<br/>  other<br/>  several<br/>  some\\\"}\"],\"styles\":\" intro_table_border\"}", "type": "table"}, {"content": "<p style=\"text-align: justify;\">Example:</p> <p style=\"text-align: justify;\"><i>Only <b>a few</b> programmers in the company forgot <b>some</b> of the validation rules. Of course, <b>most</b> of them are no longer with us.</i></p>", "type": "text"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Zero conditional in affirmative", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "We use <strong>zero conditional</strong> when we know the result of a condition or action will always be the same. ", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "The structure for the zero conditional is:</br> <div class=\"center_text\"><strong>If + present simple + present simple</strong> </br></br></div  <span class=\"italic\">Example: <br/><i><strong>If</strong> you <strong>enter</strong> data incorrectly<strong>, </strong>errors <strong>occur</strong>.</i> </br></br></span>   Notice that after the conditional or if clause (If the data\u2026) you need to place a comma to separate both ideas. </br></br>  You can also change the order of the two clauses without changing the meaning of the sentence. In this case, there is no need of a comma to separate both ideas. </br></br> Example:<br/><i> Errors<strong> occur if</strong> you <strong>enter</strong> data incorrectly. </i>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "When users enter data into any data field of a program or app, the data validation rules try to identify the errors the users are making.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "If a person misreads what is there on the source document or mishears what other person is telling him, this kind of errors surely occur.", "type": "example"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"content": "double-check ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to verify; to recheck", "type": "definition"}, {"content": " (verb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: The easiest way to avoid them would be to double-check whatever you enter into a system.  ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n3le03ob05aud01", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "misread ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to read inaccurately ", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(verb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: If a person misreads what is there on the source document or mishears what other person is telling him, this kind of errors surely occur.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n3le03ob05aud02", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "printout ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "a printed document", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: you may print out a copy of the data and compare the printout to the original paper document.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n3le03ob05aud03", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "source document ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "a document to be transcribed to a word processor or to the memory bank of a computer", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: If a person misreads what is there on the source document or mishears what other person is telling him, this kind of errors surely occur. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n3le03ob05aud04", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "transposition ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "the act of reversing the order or place of something", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: There are also transposition errors. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n3le03ob05aud05", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "validation rule ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "standard used in the process of data validation", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: The amount of errors the system can identify depends on the validation rules the software developer sets when he develops the app.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "enpe7n3le03ob05aud06", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Search for information about validation.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Identify some common problems when validating.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Write down a paragraph explaining what one should do when facing those problems", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Share your paragraph with the class and discuss about it. ", "type": "text"}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"content": "I search for information about validation.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I identify common problems when validating.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I write a paragraph explaining what people should do when facing those problems.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I share information and discuss about the topic. ", "type": "text"}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}], "type": "sena", "labs": [{"exercises": [{"questions": [], "template_override": 1, "stimuli_ids": [0], "_data": [{"contents": [{"content": "I can talk about common issues in data validation.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "I can describe how to avoid problems in validation.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "I can identify common problems in validation.", "type": "Learning Outcomes"}, {"content": "In your opinion, what are the most common problems people find during the data validation processes? ", "type": "Free Header Item"}, {"content": 